From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(resource-packs): read complete server archives

Some server archives put their manifest and assets beneath one pack directory. Select that root only when unambiguous, prefer an existing root manifest, and normalize repeated Windows separators before resolving assets. Minehut and Geyser recordings exercise those layouts.

Enchanted advertises a valid 1,251,017-byte ZIP with 58 stored files using data descriptors. ZipInputStream rejects that layout. Read the central directory through the existing Commons Compress dependency and an in-memory seekable channel, preserving physical entry order, size checks, and CRC verification. This also avoids scanning for descriptor signatures inside nested ZIP payloads.

Archive regressions cover nested roots, root preference, Windows paths, stored descriptors with embedded ZIP signatures, and corrupted entry data. The private Enchanted download reproduces the old failure and loads all 58 files with this reader. Commons documents the seekable reader behavior at https://commons.apache.org/proper/commons-compress/zip .

The central directory size bounds each read. A server archive can declare a one-byte entry whose DEFLATE data expands to gigabytes, and reading the whole stream before the size check exhausts the heap. Read at most one byte past the declared size, as the native source decoder does, and reject unknown sizes or sizes beyond a Java array. The regression declares one byte for a stream that expands past Integer.MAX_VALUE.

Bound the whole archive and its download as well. A server can send
honest entries that each pass the size checks but together exhaust the
heap, declare a 2 GiB pack in any number of RESOURCE_PACK_DATA_INFO
packets, or serve an endless HTTP response from its pack CDN URL. Check
the declared entry sizes before inflating and reject archives that
expand beyond 1 GiB. Count every pack of a connection, from the game
protocol or HTTP, against a shared 1 GiB budget before allocating it,
and read an HTTP response only up to the length its HEAD request
reported. The largest recorded server pack is a 65 MB archive that
converts to about 110 MB, so both limits stay far above real packs. A string search of the native
1.26.60.5 client found pack size fields and disk space errors, but no
pack size limit to match. Regressions cover the expanded limit, the
shared budget with replaced and invalid packs, and a chunked response
longer than expected.

Bound the whole archive and its download as well. A server can send
honest entries that each pass the size checks but together exhaust the
heap, declare a 2 GiB pack in any number of RESOURCE_PACK_DATA_INFO
packets, or serve an endless HTTP response from its pack CDN URL. Check
the declared entry sizes before inflating and reject archives that
expand beyond 1 GiB. Count every pack of a connection, from the game
protocol or HTTP, against a shared 1 GiB budget before allocating it,
and read an HTTP response only up to the length its HEAD request
reported. The largest recorded server pack is a 65 MB archive that
converts to about 110 MB, so both limits stay far above real packs. A string search of the native
1.26.60.5 client found pack size fields and disk space errors, but no
pack size limit to match. Regressions cover the expanded limit, the
shared budget with replaced and invalid packs, and a chunked response
longer than expected.

Request the archive without content coding. The downloader asked for
gzip and deflate, but HttpURLConnection does not decode them, so a CDN
that compressed its response returned bytes that failed the length check
or the ZIP parse, and the pack fell back to the slower game-protocol
download. Pack archives are already compressed, so identity coding keeps
the HEAD length equal to the bytes read and costs little bandwidth. The
regression serves gzip only to clients that accept it.

